 Memory search and region compare, through the key handler the console
 actually calls.

 These are the last two commands the debugger design specifies and the
 console did not have. `Address Lookup` is here too, and it was worse than
 missing: it was in the menu with NO dispatch arm at all, so the key fell
 through to the address evaluator and answered `expr: unknown name addr`.

 THE ARGUMENT COUNT WAS THE INTERESTING PART. The design writes these as
 `dbg mem.s <addr> <bytes>` and `dbg mem.cmp <a1> <a2> <len>`, two and
 three arguments against a console that dispatches one line. The console's
 own idiom settles it: an expression moves the cursor, a command acts
 there, and the window is the dump length -- the rule `watch` already
 uses. A search is then a pattern at the cursor and a compare is the
 cursor against one other address. One argument each.

 The two arguments are READ DIFFERENTLY, which is the whole reason the
 pending flag is a variant and not a Boolean. A search pattern is TEXT and
 is parsed here as hex digit pairs; a compare address is an EXPRESSION, so
 `$ + 0x100` works for the second region exactly as it does for the
 cursor. The test asks the compare with an expression for that reason.

 THE BUFFERS ARE CONTROLLED AND ZEROED. `alloc-bytes` does not zero what
 it returns, and a search over uninitialised heap would find the pattern
 or not depending on what the previous allocation left. Zeroing also makes
 the not-found case mean something: the window is known to be clear except
 for what this test writes into it.

 The `addr` line answers `<unknown 0x...>`: the emitter writes a MAP1 into
 every binary with named functions, so `map1-find` finds the map and the
 lookup misses inside it. This paragraph used to say `<no debug info>` and
 that no symbol map existed here, which was true only while `map1-find`
 was broken; main 11552 fixed it and the pin was not moved with it.

 We say:

Section: Fixtures

  bare-console : UefiConsoleState
  bare-console = UefiConsoleState {
    sys-table = 0,
    con-out = 0,
    con-in = 0,
    rows = 25,
    cols = 80,
    cursor-row = 0,
    cursor-col = 0
  }

  bare-disk : DiskFactStore
  bare-disk = DiskFactStore { superblock = empty-superblock, mem = fact-store-empty, base = 0, limit = 0 }

  bare-keys : Keypair
  bare-keys = generate-keypair (sha256 (text-to-bytes "codex-dev-mem-find-test"))

 The buffers are 512 bytes because the dump length is 256 and both the
 search window and the compare run that far from the cursor. A shorter
 buffer would read past what this test owns.

  buf-len : Integer = 512

  zero-fill : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  zero-fill (buf) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then 0
   else let w = poke-byte buf i 0
   in zero-fill buf (i + 1) n

Section: Reporting

  report : Text, DevConsoleState -> [Console] Nothing
  report (label) (s) = act
    print-line-uni (label & ": " & s.status)
  end

Section: Body

 The needle is three bytes at a known offset, and the readings are chosen
 so no single wrong answer satisfies them. A search that always answered
 "found at the cursor" would fail the offset; one that always answered
 "not found" would fail the hit; one that matched only the first byte
 would find the decoy at +16, which shares that byte and differs in the
 second.

  opening : [Console, Device.Port, Device.Block] Nothing = act
    let a = alloc-bytes buf-len
    in let za = zero-fill a 0 buf-len
    in let b = alloc-bytes buf-len
    in let zb = zero-fill b 0 buf-len

    in let n1 = poke-byte a 64 222
    in let n2 = poke-byte a 65 173
    in let n3 = poke-byte a 66 190
    in let d1 = poke-byte a 16 222
    in let d2 = poke-byte a 17 1

    in let s0 = dev-console-init bare-console bare-disk bare-keys
    in let d0 = dispatch-main-menu "debug" s0
    in act

      at <- handle-console-input d0 (show a)
      report "cursor-to-buffer" at

      f0 <- handle-console-input at "mem.s"
      report "search-armed" f0

      f1 <- handle-console-input f0 "deadbe"
      report "found-past-the-decoy" f1

      f2 <- handle-console-input f1 "mem.s"
      report "search-armed-again" f2

      f3 <- handle-console-input f2 "c0ffee"
      report "not-found" f3

      f4 <- handle-console-input f3 "mem.s"
      report "armed-for-odd" f4

      f5 <- handle-console-input f4 "abc"
      report "refused-odd-length" f5

      f6 <- handle-console-input f5 "mem.s"
      report "armed-for-junk" f6

      f7 <- handle-console-input f6 "zz"
      report "refused-not-hex" f7

 The compare pair is the point of the second half. The second buffer is
 still entirely zero, so the first difference against the first buffer is
 the DECOY at +16, not the needle at +64 -- the decoy is written earlier
 in the buffer and a compare reports the first difference, not the most
 interesting one. That is worth asserting exactly: an implementation that
 reported the last difference, or the largest, would pass a test that only
 demanded "they differ".

      c0 <- handle-console-input f7 "mem.cmp"
      report "compare-armed" c0

      c1 <- handle-console-input c0 (show b)
      report "differ-at-first-change" c1

      w1 <- handle-console-input c1 (show (b + 256))
      report "cursor-moved-to-b-tail" w1

      c2 <- handle-console-input w1 "mem.cmp"
      report "compare-armed-again" c2

      c3 <- handle-console-input c2 (show (a + 256))
      report "identical-tails" c3

      c4 <- handle-console-input c3 "mem.cmp"
      report "armed-for-bad-expr" c4

      c5 <- handle-console-input c4 "nosuch + 1"
      report "compare-cancelled" c5

      c6 <- handle-console-input c5 "$ + 1"
      report "next-line-is-an-address-again" c6

      ad <- handle-console-input c6 "addr"
      report "address-lookup-answers" ad
    end
  end
